        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Motor type is the specification that decides how long an electric pressure washer lasts, and it is the one most listings omit. Universal motors use brushes, run loud and hot, fade partway through long sessions, and give a few seasons of regular service. Induction motors have no brushes, run cool and quiet, and last for thousands of hours.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            When the listing will not say, weight tells you. An induction motor needs more copper and steel for the same output, so a machine under about 35 pounds claiming high pressure is almost certainly universal. Price is the other tell, and so is silence itself: brands that fitted an induction motor advertise it, because it is what they are charging for.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Neither is wrong. For a couple of short sessions a year, a universal motor is a rational purchase and the saving is real. For regular use, long sessions, or a machine you want to be quiet enough to actually reach for, buy induction. Then protect whichever you chose: never run it in bypass, feed it a short wide hose from a fully open spigot, and put pump antifreeze through it before winter.
          </p>
        </div>


